{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1528002-the-vampire-the-dark-ages-community-errata-and-faq-thread","id":1528002,"title":"The Vampire: the Dark Ages community errata and FAQ thread","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Vampire: The Masquerade"],"posts":[{"date":"2024-01-05T02:05:13-08:00","id":1528002,"title":"<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/icons/icon3.png\" width=\"16\" height=\"16\" alt=\"\" /> The Vampire: the Dark Ages community errata and FAQ thread","content":"Recently there have been quite a few threads and posts on rules in the 20th anniversary edition of <i>Vampire: the Dark Ages</i>. The issue is that the ruleset has a fair number of rules ambiguities, as well as some general weirdness as a holdover from previous editions. Rather than have them spread between various threads, it would be useful to combine all queries into a single thread. Once we have the material for a decent set of errata and an FAQ I will format it up into a nice little pdf, then post it for free download on the <i>Storyteller's Vault</i>.<br /> <br /> Let's start with something seemingly (but not actually) straightforward: the Ambidextrous Merit. This is one of these abilities that you tend to get in games, because you have details like hit locations in combat, so if you are right handed, and get your right hand chopped off, it is quite noticeable. You don't get this kind of granularity in the Storyteller system, so what exactly does this Merit do?<br /> <br /> Current wording is:<div style=\"margin-left:40px\"><i>Ambidextrous (1 point): Whether by training or a gift of birth, you are equally adept with both hands. You must still split actions as normal, but you do not incur any additional penalties for taking actions with your non-dominant hand.</i></div> <br /> There's a bit of explanation in prior editions. In <i>Dark Ages: Vampire</i> the wording is:<div style=\"margin-left:40px\"><i>Ambidextrous (1-pt Merit): You have a high degree of off-hand dexterity, and you can perform tasks with the &quot;wrong&quot; hand at no penalty. You must still use the rules for taking multiple actions, but you do not suffer a difficulty penalty if, say, you use two weapons or are forced to use your off hand.</i></div> <br /> Which is a bit better. And the original edition of <i>Vampire: the Dark Ages</i> has:<div style=\"margin-left:40px\"><i>Ambidextrous (1 pt Merit): You have a high degree of off-hand dexterity, and can perform tasks with the &quot;wrong&quot; hand at no penalty. The normal penalty for using both hands at once to perform different tasks (such as fighting with a weapon in each hand) is a +1 difficulty for the &quot;right&quot; hand and a +3 difficulty for the other hand.</i></div> <br /> So ... go back to 1996 and you actually have something resembling rules for this, but it's still problematic as, effectively, you have something resembling core rules hidden away within the section on Merits and Flaws. It would be better to actually have a rule within the combat section, along the lines of:<div style=\"margin-left:40px\"><i>Every character has a preferred hand. If, for whatever reason, a character is forced to use his or her off-hand in combat, any attacks with that hand are made at +3 difficulty.</i></div> <br /> But ... is it really a good idea to add a rule to the game, just so that a 1-point Merit actually has function?<br /> <br /> It gets more complex, though. One of the functions of Merits and Flaws is to add granular depth to characters which are not otherwise covered by the rules. Part of choosing Merits and Flaws is to make a declaration as to how you want your character to function within a chronicle. One of the weird bits of game design is you end up with Enemy as a Flaw, when actually it's a Merit. So, choosing Ambidextrous as a Merit is a statement to the Storyteller that, occasionally, you might want to showcase this aspect of your character. Which is great with most Merits and Flaws, but doesn't really work with Ambidextrous (really, we would have a whole essay about the narrative value of Merits and Flaws, but probably best not to). <br /> <br /> The easy solution? Cutting Ambidextrous entirely from the game. It makes the life of the storyteller that little bit easier if he or she doesn't have to worry about irregular rules like this.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/317-james_willoughby","name":"James_Willoughby","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar317_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"297"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-06T12:58:32-08:00","id":1528122,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>James_Willoughby</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1528002-the-vampire-the-dark-ages-community-errata-and-faq-thread#post1528002\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Recently there have been quite a few threads and posts on rules in the 20th anniversary edition of <i>Vampire: the Dark Ages</i>. The issue is that the ruleset has a fair number of rules ambiguities, as well as some general weirdness as a holdover from previous editions. Rather than have them spread between various threads, it would be useful to combine all queries into a single thread. Once we have the material for a decent set of errata and an FAQ I will format it up into a nice little pdf, then post it for free download on the <i>Storyteller's Vault</i>.<br /> <br /> Let's start with something seemingly (but not actually) straightforward: the Ambidextrous Merit. This is one of these abilities that you tend to get in games, because you have details like hit locations in combat, so if you are right handed, and get your right hand chopped off, it is quite noticeable. You don't get this kind of granularity in the Storyteller system, so what exactly does this Merit do?<br /> <br /> Current wording is:<div style=\"margin-left:40px\"><i>Ambidextrous (1 point): Whether by training or a gift of birth, you are equally adept with both hands. You must still split actions as normal, but you do not incur any additional penalties for taking actions with your non-dominant hand.</i></div><br /> There's a bit of explanation in prior editions. In <i>Dark Ages: Vampire</i> the wording is:<div style=\"margin-left:40px\"><i>Ambidextrous (1-pt Merit): You have a high degree of off-hand dexterity, and you can perform tasks with the &quot;wrong&quot; hand at no penalty. You must still use the rules for taking multiple actions, but you do not suffer a difficulty penalty if, say, you use two weapons or are forced to use your off hand.</i></div><br /> Which is a bit better. And the original edition of <i>Vampire: the Dark Ages</i> has:<div style=\"margin-left:40px\"><i>Ambidextrous (1 pt Merit): You have a high degree of off-hand dexterity, and can perform tasks with the &quot;wrong&quot; hand at no penalty. The normal penalty for using both hands at once to perform different tasks (such as fighting with a weapon in each hand) is a +1 difficulty for the &quot;right&quot; hand and a +3 difficulty for the other hand.</i></div><br /> So ... go back to 1996 and you actually have something resembling rules for this, but it's still problematic as, effectively, you have something resembling core rules hidden away within the section on Merits and Flaws. It would be better to actually have a rule within the combat section, along the lines of:<div style=\"margin-left:40px\"><i>Every character has a preferred hand. If, for whatever reason, a character is forced to use his or her off-hand in combat, any attacks with that hand are made at +3 difficulty.</i></div><br /> But ... is it really a good idea to add a rule to the game, just so that a 1-point Merit actually has function?<br /> <br /> It gets more complex, though. One of the functions of Merits and Flaws is to add granular depth to characters which are not otherwise covered by the rules. Part of choosing Merits and Flaws is to make a declaration as to how you want your character to function within a chronicle. One of the weird bits of game design is you end up with Enemy as a Flaw, when actually it's a Merit. So, choosing Ambidextrous as a Merit is a statement to the Storyteller that, occasionally, you might want to showcase this aspect of your character. Which is great with most Merits and Flaws, but doesn't really work with Ambidextrous (really, we would have a whole essay about the narrative value of Merits and Flaws, but probably best not to).<br /> <br /> The easy solution? Cutting Ambidextrous entirely from the game. It makes the life of the storyteller that little bit easier if he or she doesn't have to worry about irregular rules like this.</div> </div> </div> </div>I agree with ditching the Ambidextrous merit. The penalty is excessive with the rule system: things that pose greater trouble impose only a +1 difficulty; +3 for using the off-hand makes no sense. It's the same as what you suffer when facing 4 enemies alone...<br /> Add to that the fact that when it comes to fighting muscular memory is what counts the most, and a fighter would have to train both hands to some extent. A fighter who has no muscular memory built up in the off-hand is not serious about his training and would probably not have more than a dot in its fighting abilities.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","name":"Hermeticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-06T23:19:20-08:00","id":1528142,"title":"","content":"Here's another issue. I has started a thread about this, but nobody replied, unfortunately.<br /> <br /> The Serpentis power <i>Echidna's Venom, </i>VDA20 p<br /> 254:<div style=\"margin-left:40px\"><i>At this level, the vampire\u2019s bite becomes venomous</i> <i>as a death adder.</i></div><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"><i>System: Spend a point of blood. The vampire\u2019s fangs</i> <i>become hollow and generate venom. Any character the</i> <i>vampire bites suffers from the venom. Affected victims suffer</i> <i>the Setite\u2019s Serpentis in lethal damage in addition to whatever</i> <i>damage the bite may have caused. Humans that survive the</i> <i>damage become paralyzed (-5 to all actions) and must succeed</i> <i>in a Stamina roll every thirty seconds for five minutes or die.</i></div> Is the lethal damage inflicted simultaneously with the aggravated one, rolling the lethal damage dice separately and giving a single soak roll for both where successes are allocated first to soak the aggravated damage and then the lethal one if all aggravated damage is absorbed?<br /> Or are there two separate damage rolls and two separate soak rolls?<br /> Is the lethal damage rolled at all, or is it an automatic addition to the damage that just needs to be soaked? With the same soak roll as the aggravated damage or with a new one?<br /> <br /> The power's strength varies a lot depending on how one answers these questions. The worst case is if it's dice of damage rolled and soaked separately from the bite's normal aggravated damage. Let's say the character has 6 dots in Serpentis: he rolls 6 dice at difficulty 6, with an average of 2.4 successes. It's not written that armour does not count (though it probably shouldn't because the venom is injected after the fangs have gone deep enough to cause aggravated damage already) and all the victim needs is 5+ dice of soak between Stamina, armour and Fortitude to take no damage on average. At this level, when characters have 6 dots in a single discipline, you're not interested in complicated ways to kill mortals who can't soak lethal, so damage against supernaturals is what counts.<br /> The other options for interpretation make it better, but I'm not sure which one to pick.<br /> \u200b\u200b\u200b<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","name":"Hermeticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-07T10:10:31-08:00","id":1528155,"title":"","content":"Herpetological Ecology Nitpick: It's rather odd for the text of a Dark Ages book focusing on Europe to mention an Australasian snake as a basis of comparison.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\ude00","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-09T09:16:21-08:00","id":1528264,"title":"","content":"What does ambidextrous do in regular Vampire 20th edition? I think if we are going to make a community FAQ we should probably lean on other books produced this edition rather than going back to the 90s. Especially given that Dark Ages is such a mess.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27618-shadowcatx","name":"ShadowcatX","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27618_2.jpg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"560"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-10T13:37:38-08:00","id":1528339,"title":"","content":"I consider most of the weapons to be errors. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/tongue.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Stick Out Tongue\" smilieid=\"5\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Freelance Writer and Storyteller's Vault contributor. Find my work here: <a href=\"http://www.storytellersvault.com/index.php?affiliate_id=17903\" target=\"_blank\">http://www.storytellersvault.com/ind...liate_id=17903</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\ude00","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1535-jackob","name":"Jackob","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1535_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"293"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-11T13:32:34-08:00","id":1528390,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ShadowcatX</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1528002-the-vampire-the-dark-ages-community-errata-and-faq-thread?p=1528264#post1528264\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What does ambidextrous do in regular Vampire 20th edition? I think if we are going to make a community FAQ we should probably lean on other books produced this edition rather than going back to the 90s. Especially given that Dark Ages is such a mess.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> <i>Vampire: the Masquerade </i>revised and twentieth anniversary editions have effectively the same wording as <i>Dark Ages: Vampire</i>. With the question of relying on sources, there is something to be said for going back to older sources. There is this phenomenon which might be called \u201crules degradation\u201d, where the first incarnation of a rule set is fairly clear, as the writers are thinking clearly about how to articulate rules, and then quality degrades in later editions, as authors have an idea of the rules in their heads, and don\u2019t pay quite as much attention to what they are writing. It is notable in how the original <i>Vampire: the Dark Ages </i>provides the clearest rules for the Ambidextrous Merit.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/317-james_willoughby","name":"James_Willoughby","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar317_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"297"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-11T13:40:36-08:00","id":1528391,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1528002-the-vampire-the-dark-ages-community-errata-and-faq-thread?p=1528155#post1528155\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Herpetological Ecology Nitpick: It's rather odd for the text of a Dark Ages book focusing on Europe to mention an Australasian snake as a basis of comparison.</div> </div> </div> </div>This is one of my petty annoyances; one should aim to write as much as possible within a medieval frame of reference. Like the use of metric measurements hundreds of years before the metric system was invented.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/317-james_willoughby","name":"James_Willoughby","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar317_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"297"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-22T01:22:01-08:00","id":1529144,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Hermeticus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1528002-the-vampire-the-dark-ages-community-errata-and-faq-thread?p=1528142#post1528142\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Here's another issue. I has started a thread about this, but nobody replied, unfortunately.<br /> <br /> The Serpentis power <i>Echidna's Venom, </i>VDA20 p<br /> 254:<div style=\"margin-left:40px\"><i>At this level, the vampire\u2019s bite becomes venomous</i> <i>as a death adder.</i></div><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"><i>System: Spend a point of blood. The vampire\u2019s fangs</i> <i>become hollow and generate venom. Any character the</i> <i>vampire bites suffers from the venom. Affected victims suffer</i> <i>the Setite\u2019s Serpentis in lethal damage in addition to whatever</i> <i>damage the bite may have caused. Humans that survive the</i> <i>damage become paralyzed (-5 to all actions) and must succeed</i> <i>in a Stamina roll every thirty seconds for five minutes or die.</i></div> <br /> Is the lethal damage inflicted simultaneously with the aggravated one, rolling the lethal damage dice separately and giving a single soak roll for both where successes are allocated first to soak the aggravated damage and then the lethal one if all aggravated damage is absorbed?<br /> Or are there two separate damage rolls and two separate soak rolls?<br /> Is the lethal damage rolled at all, or is it an automatic addition to the damage that just needs to be soaked? With the same soak roll as the aggravated damage or with a new one?<br /> <br /> The power's strength varies a lot depending on how one answers these questions. The worst case is if it's dice of damage rolled and soaked separately from the bite's normal aggravated damage. Let's say the character has 6 dots in Serpentis: he rolls 6 dice at difficulty 6, with an average of 2.4 successes. It's not written that armour does not count (though it probably shouldn't because the venom is injected after the fangs have gone deep enough to cause aggravated damage already) and all the victim needs is 5+ dice of soak between Stamina, armour and Fortitude to take no damage on average. At this level, when characters have 6 dots in a single discipline, you're not interested in complicated ways to kill mortals who can't soak lethal, so damage against supernaturals is what counts.<br /> The other options for interpretation make it better, but I'm not sure which one to pick.<br /> \u200b\u200b</div> </div> </div> </div>I have been pondering this one for ages, as, in rules terms, it's a real mess.<br /> <br /> The very first question is as to what is causing the damage? Because the game rules on poison and Cainites (which have been fairly consistent between editions) is that poison only effects Cainites when consumed within the blood of a vessel. So, you don't have to fear getting bitten by a snake, as you don't have a functional circulatory system. So, should this Discipline power cause damage to Cainites? I suppose that we will say that it does, because the power is otherwise very weak (and we can say that, as a supernatural power, it transgresses normal limitations on poison) but it's still a bit inconsistent with other rules.<br /> <br /> The second issue is, as you note, the manner in which damage is inflicted. Damage caused by a Discipline power is not necessarily in damage dice, but can be in levels of damage (such as in the Quietus powers Dagon's Call and Baal's Caress). The correct language for the game rules is that a Discipline power caused 'x levels' or 'x dice' of damage, whilst Echidna's Venom does neither.<br /> <br /> Usefully, this is a repurposed (and renamed) Serpentis power from elsewhere in the game; the original rules are as follows:<div style=\"margin-left:40px\"><i>The Kindred expends one blood point, and in one turn his fangs become hollow, more slender and venomous. The vampire injects venom when he bites. He must still grapple with the victim to deliver a bite attack, and the bite does the usual amount of damage; the venom, however, kills mortals within one minute. Bitten vampires or other supernaturally resilient creatures suffer (10 \u2013 victim\u2019s Stamina and Fortitude) levels of aggravated damage over the course of five minutes.</i>\u200b</div> <br /> Which is a lot better, as it's clearer. I would suggest that the rules system of <i>Echidna's Venom</i> is replaced with the above. It's pretty nasty, as it's almost certain death for Cainites who don't have many dots in Stamina or Fortitude (remember, the original bite attack is probably going to cause some damage), but at least the rules are fairly straightforward to understand.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/317-james_willoughby","name":"James_Willoughby","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar317_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"297"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-22T12:02:13-08:00","id":1529163,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>James_Willoughby</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1528002-the-vampire-the-dark-ages-community-errata-and-faq-thread?p=1529144#post1529144\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I have been pondering this one for ages, as, in rules terms, it's a real mess.<br /> <br /> The very first question is as to what is causing the damage? Because the game rules on poison and Cainites (which have been fairly consistent between editions) is that poison only effects Cainites when consumed within the blood of a vessel. So, you don't have to fear getting bitten by a snake, as you don't have a functional circulatory system. So, should this Discipline power cause damage to Cainites? I suppose that we will say that it does, because the power is otherwise very weak (and we can say that, as a supernatural power, it transgresses normal limitations on poison) but it's still a bit inconsistent with other rules.<br /> <br /> The second issue is, as you note, the manner in which damage is inflicted. Damage caused by a Discipline power is not necessarily in damage dice, but can be in levels of damage (such as in the Quietus powers Dagon's Call and Baal's Caress). The correct language for the game rules is that a Discipline power caused 'x levels' or 'x dice' of damage, whilst Echidna's Venom does neither.<br /> <br /> Usefully, this is a repurposed (and renamed) Serpentis power from elsewhere in the game; the original rules are as follows:<div style=\"margin-left:40px\"><i>The Kindred expends one blood point, and in one turn his fangs become hollow, more slender and venomous. The vampire injects venom when he bites. He must still grapple with the victim to deliver a bite attack, and the bite does the usual amount of damage; the venom, however, kills mortals within one minute. Bitten vampires or other supernaturally resilient creatures suffer (10 \u2013 victim\u2019s Stamina and Fortitude) levels of aggravated damage over the course of five minutes.</i></div><br /> Which is a lot better, as it's clearer. I would suggest that the rules system of <i>Echidna's Venom</i> is replaced with the above. It's pretty nasty, as it's almost certain death for Cainites who don't have many dots in Stamina or Fortitude (remember, the original bite attack is probably going to cause some damage), but at least the rules are fairly straightforward to understand.</div> </div> </div> </div>The repurposed form (which \u0130 think is how it is in V20) is clearer, but \u0130 still see it as problematic: how does soaking work there? Levels of damage are usually soakable, like those from fire and sunlight. Here the damage is already reduced by Stamina + Fortitude, and then it's soaked again through Fortitude (or Stamina+Fortitude if you use VDA20 Serpentis 3), but since the damage is inflicted over the course of 5 minutes, it's unclear when the damage comes in exactly and when the soak roll is done.<br /> Aside from clarity issues, \u0130 still feel it's underpowered for a level 6 power, particularly by comparison with analogous powers like VDA20 Path of the Twilight Garden 2 (Kiss of the Dark Mother, \u0130 think it's called) and 3 and Quietus Cruscitus 2, 3 and 4. True, it's cheaper blood points-wise, but it's also so &quot;soft&quot; that \u0130 would probably not spend 25/35 xp on it. <br /> \u0130'd much rather have a simpler power like: &quot;spend 1 blood point. Your next bite inflicts extra dice of damage equal to your Serpentis rating.&quot; This can be justified as either venom or extra biting strength (like crocodiles), and both fit since Apep is described as a crocodile at times. Still weaker than Kiss of the Dark Mother, but that's an OP power anyway, especially for clans with Potence in-clan. Would be worse with Serpentis 2 ability to bite without grappling.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","name":"Hermeticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-26T17:57:50-07:00","id":1536321,"title":"","content":"Whew! glad I found this; I didn't want to start a new-thread. So, did I read that right? On page 253, of Vampire: Dark Ages (2002), did they state that, while vampires can soak lethal, mortals can't? <br /> <br /> Did I miss that out of V:tM?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;I have all the power in the world to give you, and all you have to do is beg&quot; -Nix<br /> <br /> &quot;There's thing about being a vampire, Frank; it takes a long fucking time to learn how do all the cool shit&quot;. -Abigail","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2521-iguazu","name":"Iguazu","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2521_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"738"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-26T19:01:25-07:00","id":1536325,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Iguazu</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1528002-the-vampire-the-dark-ages-community-errata-and-faq-thread?p=1536321#post1536321\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Whew! glad I found this; I didn't want to start a new-thread. So, did I read that right? On page 253, of Vampire: Dark Ages (2002), did they state that, while vampires can soak lethal, mortals can't?<br /> <br /> Did I miss that out of V:tM?</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't know about the edition from 2002, but in VDA20 that's right. Vampires and ghouls soak bashing and lethal with Stamina; vampires halve bashing, rounding down; normal humans soak bashing with Stamina, but not lethal; vampires take aggravated damage and soak it with Fortitude, while humans and ghoul consider aggravated damage as lethal. Armour works for all three: bashing, lethal and aggravated, but not for vampires against sunlight and fire.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","name":"Hermeticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"431"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}